id: lava-environment
name: Lava Environment
kind: battlefield
environment: true
hardnessMin: 3
hardnessMax: 3
qualities: aflame
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Molten ground. Ending your turn standing in it is a stack of Broken until your next
  turn begins, and it is Aflame. Hardness Rank 3.
text: >
  Lava Environment: A harsh environment where the ground is molten and excessively hot,
  but not enough to be completely submerged.

  Hardness Rank: 3

  Effects: This Battle Environment has multiple effects:

  * This Environment possesses the Aflame Environmental Quality.

  * If you end your Turn in this Battle Environment, gain a stack of the Broken Combat
  Condition until the start of your next turn.

---

# Hardness Rank 3 and nothing to pick, which is odd for molten rock and is what the entry
# says: "metal or more resilient stone". Being thrown into lava hurts like being thrown
# into a wall, and the burning is a separate rule.
#
# --- Ending your turn in it --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "If you end your Turn in this Battle Environment, gain a stack of the Broken Combat
# Condition until the start of your next turn."
#
# The whole condition is "in this Battle Environment", and that is not written below: being
# gathered is what standing in it means. What is left is the Moment - the end of your turn -
# and the stack it hands out.
#
# "Until the start of your next turn", which is the edge Brace already uses. A start edge is
# never ahead of you during your own turn, so it is always the next one: end your turn in
# the lava and it lifts as your next turn begins. Stand in it for three turns and you have
# three stacks, because each one is put on at the end of a turn and taken off at the start
# of the following one - which is a stack at a time, and Broken caps at three.
#
# --- The Aflame Environmental Quality -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Carried in the header and read by nobody. Environmental Qualities are their own list and
# it has not arrived yet - the same shape a Feature's Qualities had before they did, and the
# same shape the Elemental Profiles have now.
#
# `qualities` rather than `quality`, because a Feature can have several and there is no
# reason to think an Environment cannot.

[automatic/end-of-turn]
gain(broken);
expires(broken, start-of-next-turn);
